I. Users Part
-------------------------------------------
-------------------------------------------
I.a) Generating dependency file during compilation
-------------------------------------------
Fortran project is known to not be very developper friendly for dependency rule automatic generation.
However, the Python executable fortdepend can allow us to build the dependency files prior to compilation.
REQUIREMENT : fortdepend requires python3
LIMITATION : it looks at matching pairs of program <name>/end program <name> and module <name>/end module <name>
	
(more info on how to install it is available here : https://pypi.org/project/fortdepend/ ) 
FOR GASTBY USER: fortdepend is already installed on the cluster but you need to load the python3 module for it to run properly.	




I.b) Compiling the Code.
-------------------------------------------
The basic idea of the compilation of NDM package is based on a primary Makefile. 
This primary Makefile gather all files to be compiled based on predefine Macros.
It also create the dependency file before compilation.
Then specific options (compiler choose, library to load, compiler flags) is inside the secondary Makefile.
All secondary Makefile are in the folder MAKE. 
By exploring few of them, one can see a sort template.

USERS ARE NOT SUPPOSED TO CHANGE THE PRIMARY MAKEFILE. 
THEY SHOULD TRY TO USE EXISTING SECONDARY MAKEFILE OR CREATE THEIR OWN ONE BASED ON THEIR ARCHITECTURE.

A first sight is given when one just enter :
make 

It will prompt a little helper of what can be appended to the make   

!make arch             build NDM where arch is one of:
!
! MACRO NEEDED PARA=1 LAMMPS=1	, ndm_lammps_mpi 		= NDN with LAMMPS with OPENMPI code with Intel Compiler on Gatsby cluster Linux arch
! MACRO NEEDED LAMMPS=1		, ndm_lammps_serial 		= NDM with LAMMPS code with Intel Compiler on Gatsby cluster Linux arch
! MACRO NEEDED LAMMPS=1		, ndm_lammps_serial_gfortran 	= NDM code with GNU Compiler on Gatsby cluster Linux arch
! MACRO NEEDED ML=1		, ndm_ml 			= NDM + MILADI code with Intel Compiler on Gatsby cluster Linux arch
! MACRO NEEDED PARA=1		, ndm_mpi 			= NDM + OPENMPI  with Intel Compiler on Gatsby cluster Linux arch
! MACRO NEEDED PARA=1		, ndm_mpi_gfortran 		= NDM with OPENMPI code with GNU Compiler on Gatsby cluster Linux arch
! 				  ndm_serial 			= NDM code with Intel Compiler on Gatsby cluster Linux arch
! 				  ndm_serial_gfortran 		= NDM code with GNU Compiler on Gatsby cluster Linux arch
! 				  template 			= TEMPLATE COPY THIS FILE WITH A NEW EXTENSION AND ASSIGN VARIABLE AT YOUR NEED
!
! make clean-arch          delete object folder and executable of arch
! make softclean-arch      only delete executable of arch
! make clean-all           delete object folder and executable of all arch
! make softclean-all       only delete executable of all arch
! make tar                 create NDM.tar.gz of src dir and packages


The basic package is the serial version of NDM which can be compiled with the following command line: 

make ndm_serial           ! use Intel compiler
or 
make ndm_serial_gfortran

Also some executable build,as mentioned above, requires the use to append the MACRO in the command line such as the openmpi version of NDM:

make ndm_mpi PARA=1
or 
make ndm_mpi_gfortran PARA=1


FOR GASTBY USER: A bash script is availabled (named GASTBY.sh which will compile all available architecture compatible on Gastby).
So one can just enter:
./GASTBY.sh	





II) Devellopers part (DEV)
-------------------------------------------
-------------------------------------------
II.a) Information regarding fortdepend
-------------------------------------------
As mentioned in the users part, fortdepend has several requirement and limitations
REQUIREMENT : fortdepend requires python3
LIMITATION : it looks at matching pairs of program <name>/end program <name> and module <name>/end module <name>

Because of the scaning of module only each source file in the project must be encapsulated in a module:  

This have pros and cons. 
One avantage is that if two subroutines A & B are inside the same module mod1 and B need a variable varA from A then external declaration in B is unnecessary  
since the variable is declared inside the module mod1.

One disavantage is that one cannot stack subroutine in a file randomly. One has to make sure that no cyclic dependency is created.


fortdepend to not react well with \#ifdef macros that contains parenthesis or }{ or space between if and def, here few exemple:
#ifdef (TOTO) should be  #ifdef TOTO
#if def TOTO  should be  #ifdef TOTO

More when logical and/or are needed, fortdepend understand only one specific format:

#if (PARA || ML) should be #if defined PARA || defined ML

II.b) Develloper restrictions for content additions.
-------------------------------------------
There is three main restriction regarding addition of content in the project :
	new files need to be encapsulated inside modules
	ifdef statement as one specific format mention above
	(Recommanded not requirement) fortran file should be *.F90 exclusively (case sensitive)

II.c) Develloper watchout for new MACRO supported.
-------------------------------------------
HERE PHONDY MACRO WILL BE USED AS A TEST FOR DEV

First of all to add a new macro which contains additionnal files such as PHONDY please try to collect then in a subdirectory to avoid any potential alteration of the main project NDM.

*******************************************
Open primary Makefile with your beloved editor
One can see the \# Definition section

Copy paste one of the present blocks starting from f77 up to OBJ variables.
change variable name for original to phondy for exemple
f90para   = $(wildcard para/*.F90) -> f90phondy   = $(wildcard phondy/*.F90)
NOTE: do not forget to change the scaned folder inside the wildcard !!

*******************************************
NOW copy paste one of the present \#ifdef 
Update names OBJ_* f90* INC_* and declare the FLAG present in the source code
NOTE: the name of the macro here does not have to be the name of the ifdef in the code check ML for instance 

*******************************************

You have added all the necessary new declaration to use the new macro PHONDY or TOTO.

NOW go to following section (new secondary Makefile) 


II.d) Develloper watchout for new secondary Makefile.
-------------------------------------------
Copy paste any of the secondary makefile that is the "closest" to your new architecture 
for phondy, the ml makefile seems a good start.

FIRST PLEASE UPDATE THE HEADER OF THE NEW SECONDARY MAKEFILE
*******************************************

SECOND, verify if compiler/linker settings are what you want.
*******************************************

THIRD, check OPTIONS variable, and change NDM_INC variable, it should reflect phondy so -DPHONDY in serial or -DPHONDY -DPARAPH in parallel version.
*******************************************

FOURTH, you might need to update the INC, PATH and LIB of some the predefined blocs.

For exemple, the MPI blocs is set to work on gastby so if you try to construct a makefile on your local machine you need to provide it INC and PATH variable as well as the mpi library of your choice.

Same thing with MKL blocs it will be completely different if you use GNU or INTEL compiler.

*******************************************
LATE THING BUT VERY IMPORTANT ONE 

UPDATE THE CODE_PATH VARIABLE 
for exemple from ndm_ml to ndm_phondy it has to change from 
CODE_PATH = ../ml::../mod/:..

to 

CODE_PATH = ../phondy::../mod/:..


NOW your setup is completed for phondy! but becareful the original files of phondy have not been encapsulated in module nor \#ifdef statement updated.




